  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A ribosome-bound pseudoknot in the HCV coding region stimulates viral growth by tuning viral translation (2025) in Cell Reports. Funding: NIGMS NIH HHS, NHGRI NIH HHS, National Institutes of Health, National Science Foundation, National Science Foundation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A ribosome-bound pseudoknot in the HCV coding region stimulates viral growth by tuning viral translation (2025) in Cell Reports is authored by Han Wan, Harim Jang and Ling Xu. Topics: Pseudoknot, Biology, RNA, Translation (biology) and Computational biology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Cell Reports study identifies a previously uncharacterized pseudoknot in the hepatitis C virus coding region that binds host ribosomes and modulates translation, offering a mechanistic picture of how RNA structure can help tune viral growth. The record shows early scholarly uptake rather than broad diffusion so far: the article has one documented downstream reuse in Europe PMC, five RRID-linked reproducibility annotations, and no citations yet in OpenAlex.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Wan, Jang, Xu, Abdallah, Gilbert, and Pyle report a ribosome-bound pseudoknot, pk1, in the hepatitis C virus coding region and show that it influences translation in a way that may help balance viral protein production with genome replication. The work stands out for pairing an unbiased proteomic strategy with targeted biochemical assays, giving it a clear experimental foundation for a previously uncharacterized regulatory RNA element.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signals in the record are about research quality and reuse. Five RRID entries indicate that the article documents key resources in a reproducible way, supporting the study\u2019s technical transparency. In addition, one downstream reuse has already been captured in Europe PMC, suggesting that the paper is beginning to circulate within the biomedical literature even though citation-based impact has not yet accrued.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Support from NIGMS, NHGRI, the National Institutes of Health, the National Science Foundation, and Howard Hughes Medical Institute situates the work within a well-resourced program in RNA biology and genome-scale methodology. The open-access publication in Cell Reports further increases accessibility for readers working on viral RNA structure, translation control, and ribosome\u2013RNA interactions.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader impact remains early and evidence is still thin. OpenAlex currently records zero citations, and the available discourse signals do not show public discussion in the sources collected here. That quiet profile does not diminish the study\u2019s scientific relevance; rather, it indicates that the article is at an early stage of dissemination, with its main contribution so far being a mechanistic advance and a reproducible experimental framework for studying structured RNA elements in RNA viruses.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">For scholars in virology and RNA structural biology, the paper\u2019s importance lies in connecting RNA architecture to a functional outcome at the level of translation control. By showing that a coding-region pseudoknot can engage ribosomes and alter viral growth, the study adds a concrete example of how structured viral RNA can act as a regulatory element, and it may help guide future work on RNA-mediated control points in hepatitis C virus and related systems.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}